<head>
  <title>ZebraIMG: Import Zebra (rf) format raster files</title>
<meta content="text/html; charset=ISO-8859-1" http-equiv="Content-Type"/></head>

<body>
<!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent al titol de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<span id="wpstitol"/>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent titol de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->

<!--Aquí inicia la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA. Aquesta part s'ha d'editar desde el document WPS (aplicacio.mps).-->
<span id="wpsopcions"/>
<!--Aquí acaba la part corresponent a les opcions de de la MSA editable de el WPS.-->

  <p>Although the MiraMon raster format requires the same resolution in both
  dimensions and, therefore, a square pixel size, nevertheless the program
  allows import of <i>RF</i> rasters with non-square pixel sides. In this case
  a bilinear geometric correction is performed to resample the original data
  to a net of equally spaced square pixels with the side equal to the smaller
  of the two dimensions of the original.</p>

  <p>The program also allows the original raster to be transformed
  geometrically to a chosen reference system. In this case it is necessary to
  identify the original reference system from amongst those reference systems
  supported by MiraMon, and to choose the output system for
  reprojection.<br/>
  It is also possible to choose not to reproject, and the program will
  document as the reference system of the output raster that system that is
  the default used by the Zebra <i>software</i>. This is an equidistant
  cylindrical projection in a spherical model; the centre can be defined in
  the users geodesic tables.</p>

  <p>This version does not support multiband input rasters. If the same raster
  can be obtained in GrADS format then this can be correctly imported with all
  the channels to MiraMon format using the module <a href="GrADSIMG.htm">GrADSIMG</a>.</p>
  <hr/>

